@TMerlini  the consumer-side rule you added is the half I missed — “unavailable relay copy = retrieval failure, not evidence of non-commitment” is exactly the asymmetry that matters, since the inverse error (treating rot as repudiation) would let anyone erase a validator’s commitments by attacking relay retention. 
 One refinement from how the production audit actually resolves these, worth folding into the normative note: the  relay_anchor  status SHOULD distinguish  recoverable  from  unrecoverable  retrieval failure. Ours serves three states —  anchored  (declared event live on declared relays),  replaced_by_newer_same_artifact  (the declared event was NIP-33-overwritten, but the surviving event commits to the  same   artifact_hash , and the status names the survivor’s event id), and  unverified . The middle state is the important one: all 9 of our overwritten entries were same-artifact re-signs, so the commitment evidence still exists on the relays — just under a different event id. A consumer that gets the survivor pointer can fetch it, verify the signature, and confirm it binds the same artifact hash — full commitment verification, recovered. Without that pointer, a naive consumer hits “retrieval failure” and stops, even though the proof is one hop away. Suggested phrasing:  where the producer can identify a surviving equivalent event, the retention status SHOULD reference it, and consumers MAY treat a verified same-artifact survivor as satisfying the commitment check. 
 The mechanism-tag adoption closes the loop neatly: the survivor-reference is itself only interpretable because the evidence blob declares  nostr-relay-publication  — a consumer knows what “event id” means in that trust model. Different anchor types will need their own recovery semantics (an on-chain commit has none to need), which is exactly why the tag had to come first.

